# Survey — a small game built on Core, written entirely in Compass.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE IS THE ENGINE'S DOOR, AND NOTHING ELSE. Every function the engine calls is here, and each
# one hands the work to the model that owns it. The engine only ever looks at `Rules`, so a game that
# put its whole self in this file would be a game in one file — the folders beside it are what the
# organizing looks like:
#
#   declare/    what the game IS, said once before the world exists
#   rules/      what happens to somebody, said every time it does
#   ranks.cm    a lookup this game owns and nothing else has an opinion about
#
# Every .cm under the scripts folder is one module, however deep it sits, so a folder is an organizing
# idea rather than a boundary. A model in one file reaches a model in another by name.

shared model Rules

    # Declaring runs once, before the world exists. Everything it says is about what the game IS, never
    # about anybody in it — there is nobody in it yet.
    public function Configure(Builder game)
        Surveyor.Declare(game);
        Land.Declare(game);
        Book.Declare(game);

        # Resting is the half of stamina nothing in the world triggers: it happens as time passes. Once
        # a second rather than once a tick, because a point a second is the rate this game wants.
        game.TickEvery(20);
    end function

    public function OnPlayerJoined(Player who)
        Surveyor.Enrol(who);
    end function

    public function OnPlayerMoved(Player who, integer fromX, integer fromY)
        Walking.Stepped(who);
    end function

    # Once for each body in the world, every twentieth tick. This is the only way a script can walk the
    # roster, which is why resting is written per-player rather than as a sweep.
    public function OnPlayerTick(Player who)
        Recovery.Rest(who);
    end function

    # `on` names whoever the verb was used on, and is blank for one offered on a square or on the HUD.
    public function OnAction(Player who, string action, string on, integer map, integer x, integer y)
        Notes.Did(who, action, on);
    end function

    # Nothing dies on a botanical survey. A refusal is a real answer rather than an absence: Core's
    # death path exists and works, and this game says it never runs.
    public string function OnMayDie(Player who, string cause)
        yield "Nobody dies out here.";
    end function

    # A dropped line is not a lost afternoon. Long enough that reconnecting puts a surveyor back where
    # they were, short enough that nobody is left standing in a meadow overnight.
    public integer function OnLinger(Player who)
        yield 30;
    end function

end model
